\f1\fs26 They fled the apartment, ran down the fire stairs to the basement, and to Cracker\'92s van.\
\'93Where can we go?\'94 Cracker asked, expecting an Airborne like Sarah to have bodyguards or safe-houses, or judges in their pockets, or pockets of money to buy their way out of this mess.\
\'93I don\'92t know!\'94 Sarah said, exasperated.\
They jumped in the van. Once out on the street, Cracker demonstrated his lack of speeding skills with screeching tyres, side drifts, and uncontrolled, teetering two-wheeling around corners.\
\'93What the hell did you steal from these people?\'94 Sarah said, agitated and frightened.\
\'93Nothing,\'94 Cracker said, as he botched a tight corner and almost cleaned up a parked vehicle.\
\'93How about this disc? It\'92s obviously important. You truly know nothing about it?\'94\
Cracker\'92s blood chilled. 
\f2\i Coincidence
\f1\i0 . Sarah registered his hesitation. \'93It\'92s nothing!\'94 Cracker defended.\
\'93What\'92s nothing?\'94 Sarah was becoming frantic.\
\'93Just some old boot leather I picked up from a dead guy on a street. 
\f2\i That
\f1\i0  nothing.\'94\
Sarah blinked, trying to process. \'93You stole a dead guy\'92s boot?\'94\
\'93No!\'94 Cracker protested, but felt ashamed anyway. \'93Look, it\'92s just a piece of junk, ok? It\'92s still down there, I think, in the footwell.\'94 Her nodded at her knees.\
Sarah hesitated, wondering what kind of gunk she\'92d get on her hands. She grimaced and held her breath while she felt around the floor. She almost missed it; it felt like part of the floor mat until she realised it was loose. Tentatively, she picked at it, got a finger nail under, and retrieved it. By nighttime streetlight, she examined it, both sides.\
\'93This can\'92t be what the fuss is about \'85 Whoah!\'94 The leathery disc in her hand shimmered a rainbow. \'93Did you see that?\'94\
\'93What?\'94 Cracker kept his eyes on the road and his hands doing manic things with the steering wheel.\
\'93It \'85 flashed.\'94\
Cracker risked a glance, saw only the daggy circle of hide he\'92d tossed aside days ago. \'93Yeah. I thought it did that before. It\'92s just a trick of the light.\'94\
Sarak flipped it over and over and tilted it at various angles, trying to catch the light again. She twisted it, just a little, testing its flexibility.\
The van crashed and pivoted over its front wheel, landed on its passenger side, slid across the street and collided with a kebab cart. Tabouleh and sauces, red and white, smeared over the van\'92s windscreen.\
Sarah, in the seat closest to the collision, didn\'92t get thrown much as the van toppled over. She would have suffered no injury at all if the Cracker\'92s flailing body hadn\'92t come down upon her.\
\'93You hit a pot hole.\'94 Sarah enunciated the accusation slowly and precisely, containing her fury.\
\'93Nope. I travel these roads every day. There\'92s no pothole back there.\'94\
\'93Then what did you hit?\'94\
\'93I dunno. Wombat?\'94 Cracker tried to grin but his jaw hurt. \'93C\'92mon. We gotta move.\'94 He lifted himself off Sarah, climbed in back, stumbled over dislodged equipment and supplies, and threw the payload doors open.\
Sarah followed. \
Around front, Cracker appraised the windscreen mess. \'93Tax! I hate garlic sauce!\'94\
Sarah looked for damage, evidence of the original collision. There appeared no damage to the front wheel, the wheel arch, or the axel, as if some titan had put the van on its side as easily as a chess piece.\
\'93C\'92mon.\'94 Cracker strode away, already some metres down the road.\
\'93Where are you going?\'94\
\'93To listen to some karaoke.\'94
